<!--
  The platform theme the window uses before Compose draws. Everything the user
  sees after the splash comes from TaffyTheme in :core:designsystem. The
  launch theme is Theme.TaffyGo.Starting (AndroidX SplashScreen); the
  activity then switches to Theme.TaffyGo, which is AppCompat so the
  language backport on API 29 to 32 can wrap it.
-->
<resources>
    <!--
      DayNight rather than the plain AppCompat parent, which is the dark one.
      Under that parent isLightTheme resolved false and every ?android:
      default with it, even on the paper theme; nothing platform-drawn appears
      while Compose is up, so it stayed invisible, but the background below is
      drawn before Compose exists and would not have.

      That background is the ground the window stands on for every frame the
      app has not painted yet. Only a cold start gets a system starting
      window: a recreation for a stored language, a rotation, a font-size or
      theme change all re-enter with no splash behind them, and the first of
      those happens on the first run that stores a language. It was
      transparent, so those frames showed the launcher, the outgoing
      screenshot or black rather than this app.

      It names splash_background because that colour already answers this
      question — what the platform paints before Compose exists — and there is
      no second answer to keep in step with the first.

      What it cannot answer is which theme the person chose. It resolves
      through the night qualifier, which is the device, and the Appearance
      setting is allowed to disagree with the device (screen SCR-407); left to
      itself this would stand a dark app on paper. It is not left to itself.
      The browser-owned Activity levels the window from the stored preference,
      before any frame exists, so this value stands only for the phase before
      that runs — a cold start, where the preference has not come off disk yet,
      the device is the best answer anyone has, and the held splash covers it.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.TaffyGo" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The platform splash (androidx.core:core-splashscreen). The activity
      launches in this theme; installSplashScreen() then switches to
      Theme.TaffyGo. The background follows the system night bit because
      stored appearance has not been read yet, and so does the icon: it is the
      one brand asset picked by a resource qualifier instead of by TaffyTheme,
      because the platform draws this window before any of this app is running
      to be asked.

      No icon background. The platform offers a disc for a mark that needs
      contrast against the window, and this one does not — the ribbon ships in
      two treatments and each is drawn for its own ground. Leaving the disc
      off also widens the circle the platform masks the icon to from 160 dp to
      192 dp, so the mark is both larger and drawn from a file authored for
      that size rather than scaled up from the launcher's padded layer.

      The icon is a raster set at five densities where the platform guide asks
      for a vector. There is no vector form of the mark in brand/ — it is a
      gradient ribbon — and the icon set is open decision OD-071, so deriving
      one here would mean inventing artwork. The densities are the interim.
    -->
    <style name="Base.Theme.TaffyGo.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/taffy_splash_icon</item>
        <!--
          Nothing animates, so there is nothing to wait for. Said out loud
          rather than left out: from API 31 the library maps the platform
          attribute onto this one and stops supplying a default of its own, so
          an absent value is an unresolved reference that merely happens to
          read as zero.
        -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.TaffyGo</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      Split from its own base so the API 33 qualifier can add one item without
      restating the rest, which is the shape the splash library uses for its
      own Base.vNN styles.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.TaffyGo.Starting" parent="Base.Theme.TaffyGo.Starting" />
</resources>
